WEBVTT

00:00:03.778 --> 00:00:10.122
When you bring a real question to this site, it helps to know what
the program stands for and where it stops.

00:00:10.244 --> 00:00:13.093
That is the job of the Program charter.

00:00:13.216 --> 00:00:20.867
It sets out the principles and responsibilities guiding One DSD
People, Access and Culture, a resource for staff of the Disability

00:00:20.867 --> 00:00:22.227
Services Division.

00:00:22.349 --> 00:00:26.854
Notice the honest framing near the top: this is a proposed charter.

00:00:26.977 --> 00:00:34.737
Applicable law, approved Department of Human Services policies and
existing decision authority continue to govern official work.

00:00:34.859 --> 00:00:39.781
The charter organizes the program around those obligations rather
than replacing them.

00:00:40.348 --> 00:00:43.080
The first article states the mission.

00:00:43.202 --> 00:00:50.838
The program exists to help staff embed equity in their work and
their workplace, connecting learning, community knowledge, evidence,

00:00:50.838 --> 00:00:54.609
practical recommendations, authorized decisions and results.

00:00:54.731 --> 00:01:03.884
It also defines what operationalizing equity means here: considering
equity in how work is designed, decided, resourced, implemented,

00:01:03.884 --> 00:01:05.698
evaluated and revised.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:09.676
Success is not measured by how many people finish a course.

00:01:09.798 --> 00:01:17.104
Instead, the charter looks for changes in barriers, access,
participation and experience, alongside evidence that someone

00:01:17.104 --> 00:01:18.973
actually followed through.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:23.307
Article two is where the program draws its firmest lines.

00:01:23.429 --> 00:01:29.507
Applicable law and binding agency requirements come before any
program guidance or learning example.

00:01:29.629 --> 00:01:34.551
Official decisions stay with the people and offices that hold the
relevant authority.

00:01:34.673 --> 00:01:42.716
And there is a rule against substitution: a practice draft is never
presented as an approved policy, a completed official review or a

00:01:42.716 --> 00:01:44.226
demonstrated result.

00:01:44.348 --> 00:01:46.692
That distinction protects you.

00:01:46.814 --> 00:01:54.541
Whatever you prepare here can strengthen your work, but it remains
clearly labeled as your preparation, not a decision someone else has

00:01:54.541 --> 00:01:55.586
already made.

00:01:56.152 --> 00:02:00.743
The operating principles describe how the work should feel in
practice.

00:02:00.866 --> 00:02:06.232
Equity is considered while decisions are still open and proposals
can still change.

00:02:06.354 --> 00:02:14.121
Before treating a problem as one person's failing, you examine the
rules, resources, incentives and decision rights around it.

00:02:14.243 --> 00:02:17.477
Affected people contribute knowledge and real influence.

00:02:17.599 --> 00:02:22.679
Accessibility and language access are design conditions, not
afterthoughts.

00:02:22.801 --> 00:02:25.946
Evidence and uncertainty stay visible.

00:02:26.068 --> 00:02:31.822
One principle deserves special attention: participation shall not
become surveillance.

00:02:31.944 --> 00:02:38.798
Your private reflection and voluntary learning are kept separate
from any scoring or monitoring of employees.

00:02:39.364 --> 00:02:48.030
Article five describes a common path that connects a work question
to evidence, analysis, options, review, action and learning from

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:48.859
results.

00:02:48.981 --> 00:02:57.118
The depth of review should match the significance, uncertainty and
likely effects of the decision, so a small question does not demand

00:02:57.118 --> 00:02:58.208
a full study.

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:05.588
Your work record should say accurately whether something is a draft,
reviewed, decided, implemented or still waiting.

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:08.943
The next article is candid about scope as well.

00:03:09.066 --> 00:03:16.642
Consultation intake and response-time commitments are excluded from
the current application, and existing specialist and decision routes

00:03:16.642 --> 00:03:18.502
stay exactly where they are.

00:03:19.069 --> 00:03:27.049
Later articles carry these commitments into policy, grants and
contracts, workforce and culture, measurement, learning, and

00:03:27.049 --> 00:03:28.258
accessibility.

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:32.591
Article twelve speaks directly to how your information is handled.

00:03:32.713 --> 00:03:39.413
Public learning, private reflection and authorized operational
information are kept in separate environments.

00:03:39.536 --> 00:03:45.184
The program explains where a draft is saved, how you can recover it
and what may be lost.

00:03:45.307 --> 00:03:52.846
It collects only what a stated purpose needs, asks you to leave case
and personnel details out of practice, and will not reuse

00:03:52.846 --> 00:03:57.873
information for an unrelated purpose without proper authority and
transparency.

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:03.464
Article thirteen is titled Ask and the judgment of responsible
people.

00:04:03.587 --> 00:04:10.494
Ask is a place to put a question in your own words and receive a
considered answer with its sources and limits.

00:04:10.617 --> 00:04:13.850
It can support research, drafting and analysis.

00:04:13.972 --> 00:04:22.062
It never makes official eligibility, personnel, legal, procurement
or other decisions that belong to the people responsible for them.

00:04:22.184 --> 00:04:27.828
Sources stay in view, and the difference between evidence and
interpretation stays clear.

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:35.971
A responsible person reviews any suggestion before it is relied on
in official work, and nothing is sent or published without explicit

00:04:35.971 --> 00:04:37.109
authorization.

00:04:37.676 --> 00:04:41.521
The final articles turn the charter on the program itself.

00:04:41.643 --> 00:04:48.995
Resources carry their source, review and version information, and
new capabilities are checked for evidence, accessibility,

00:04:48.995 --> 00:04:52.343
information handling and boundaries before release.

00:04:52.466 --> 00:05:00.726
Article sixteen is plain about status: the charter is not treated as
adopted until the necessary authorized review takes place.

00:05:00.848 --> 00:05:08.441
Until then, the priority is strengthening trust, sources, useful
work records and the connection between learning and practice.

00:05:08.563 --> 00:05:15.091
A good next step is to open the article closest to your own work and
read it with a current decision in mind.
